{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CNucXkoNYazFayXPRZiEFH","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n7:## North star\n17:## The mental model\n29:## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n64:## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n72:## Hard rules\n82:## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n120:## Frame order\n140:## Systems in more depth\n142:### Views and resolution\n150:### Scenes\n155:### Camera\n163:### Players (two to four on one machine)\n169:### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n178:### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n190:### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n222:### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n242:### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n249:### Body (ground-plane physics)\n255:### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n267:### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n273:### Attack (melee timing)\n280:### Bullets and patterns\n286:### Humanoid\n297:### Audio\n305:### Text and UI\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n319:### Lighting\n328:### Errors and warnings\n338:### Testing without a browser\n357:### The handoff package\n360:### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n367:### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\n374:### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n402:## Genre recipes\n606:## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n631:## Where to find more: animation and examples\n648:## Performance\n656:## Checklist before handing a game back","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"e93d33b7-9813-4e32-a011-d24cd81e02d7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:56:49.229Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1:# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n7:## North star\n17:## The mental model\n29:## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n64:## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n72:## Hard rules\n82:## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n120:## Frame order\n140:## Systems in more depth\n142:### Views and resolution\n150:### Scenes\n155:### Camera\n163:### Players (two to four on one machine)\n169:### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n178:### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n190:### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n222:### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n242:### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n249:### Body (ground-plane physics)\n255:### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n267:### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n273:### Attack (melee timing)\n280:### Bullets and patterns\n286:### Humanoid\n297:### Audio\n305:### Text and UI\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n319:### Lighting\n328:### Errors and warnings\n338:### Testing without a browser\n357:### The handoff package\n360:### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n367:### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\n374:### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n402:## Genre recipes\n606:## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n631:## Where to find more: animation and examples\n648:## Performance\n656:## Checklist before handing a game back","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CNucXkoNYazFayXPRZiEFH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghNbS6VoWCUwGVWuKZir","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HS5phLLkzfGzEFVz8xBRbD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453/.src/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff; sed -n 44,140p AI_GUIDE.md; sed -n 150,170p AI_GUIDE.md; sed -n 286,360p AI_GUIDE.md","description":"Read key AI_GUIDE sections"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":890,"cache_read_input_tokens":178425,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":890},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"135d43c7-bd38-44e7-a78d-f697483408ee","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:56:54.760Z","request_id":"req_011CghNbRqdVWDUpfWSLULhq","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01HS5phLLkzfGzEFVz8xBRbD":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453/.src/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff; sed -n 44,140p AI_GUIDE.md; sed -n 150,170p AI_GUIDE.md; sed -n 286,360p AI_GUIDE.md","description":"Read key AI_GUIDE sections"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HS5phLLkzfGzEFVz8xBRbD","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (33.3KB). Full output saved to: /home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261103-225453--x8def6b-work/e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184/tool-results/bjsux84eg.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\nPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n\n**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"a2945ef4-af71-4fdb-990a-b83d1069efda","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:56:54.780Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\nPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n\n**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n- *Bare rooms.* Put 3-8 props on every screen with `r.prop`: candles, chandeliers and stained-glass windows in castles; crates, barrels, signs and lamps in towns; trees, bushes, flowers, rocks and grass outdoors; crystals, bones, skulls, cobwebs and vines in caves; awnings, lanterns, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and fire drums on streets; sandbags, cactus and palms in deserts; mushrooms in forests. Tint props with `color` so they match the palette, and set `E.style.propSize = 1.4` when heroes are size 1.2+ so a door is taller than the hero.\n- *Weak hits.* Every hit gets hit-stop (`game.freeze(.04-.08)`), shake, sparks and a sound. Every death and explosion gets `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` and, for big ones, `game.flash`. Pickups get `particles.glints`.\n- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n- *Muddy lighting.* Use lights for mood, not darkness: a few warm lights (`r.prop(..., { light: true })`) over an ambient of .4-.6; GPU lighting in top-down dungeons.\n\n**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n- *Symphony of the Night:* `res: 'ps1'`, the `'castle-night'` backdrop, PlatformMap `stone` tiles with `moss` and `brick` walls, `'back'` tiles for the rear wall, candles and candelabras every few tiles, chandeliers, stained-glass `window` props, banners and statues. The hero is `build: 'heroic'`, `outfit: 'coat'`, `hair: 'long'`, `cape`, pale skin and silver hair, and the sword chops in the screen plane by itself in the side view; enemies are skeletons (`build: 'skeleton'`, add `outfit: 'robe'` and a staff for a lich), bats (`Blob` with `wings: true, mouth: 'fangs'`), cobwebs and vines on the walls.\n- *Metal Slug:* the `'desert'` backdrop, `ground` and `metal` tiles, crates, barrels, sandbags, palms and cactus, `broken` pillars, `particles.explosion` everywhere (with `particles.ground` set so debris bounces on platforms), `bulky` soldiers with `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` while firing, and screen shake on every blast.\n- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` walls under `tiles` roofs, `hedge` walls with `leaves` tops and `rock` cliffs with `grass` tops, trees, bushes, flowers, fences and mushrooms as props, `chibi` characters with `face: { eyes: 'big' }` and bright palettes, rabbites (`Blob` with `ears: 'rabbit', feet: true`), and dialog boxes with portraits.\n- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +12), and a soft camera `lead`.\n- *Final Fight / Streets of Rage:* the `brawler` view, a long street TileMap with brick and `plaster` building walls, awnings, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and burning fire drums along the sidewalk, `bulky` heroes and thugs with varied palettes, and big impacts.\n\n## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\nBroken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n- **Moves, not raw specs.** `new E.Attack('overhead')`, `new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'])`. Every move in `E.MOVES` winds up, strikes with a lunge, step, lean and twist, follows through, holds, and eases back to the stance. It never sweeps backwards through its own arc.\n- **A move set per character.** A knight gets `overhead`, `thrust` and `spin`; a mage `cast`; a rogue `jab`, `thrust` and `backslash`; a monk `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse` and `flyingkick`; a brute `haymaker`, `bash` and `sweep`; a beast `claw`. Two heroes must never share one swing.\n- **Stances and poses between attacks.** Brawlers walk with `stance: 'guard'`, knights with `stance: 'ready'`. Win with `pose: 'cheer'`, cast with `'cast'`, block with `'block'` (weapons) or `'guard'` (fists), duck with `'crouch'`, taunt with `'hips'` or `'wave'`, fall with `down`, die with `'die'` (stagger, knees, topple), and climb ladders with `climb: true`. Faces act too: `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'`, and a Dialog portrait's mouth moves while its line types.\n- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n- **Check the motion.** `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game.html#scene --steps \"... rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records the swing frame by frame. Look for the wind-up, the strike and a clean settle. Add `--seed 1` for a repeatable strip, and `--compare before.html` (a copy of the page from before your change) to see exactly which pixels your change moved.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n2. **Draw only with engine primitives** (`E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props, `r.*` helpers). `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText`, canvas gradients and scaled `drawImage` anti-alias and break the pixel look. Translucency goes through the engine helpers (`px.blend`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc`, `r.shadow`): stepped alpha with blend modes by default (PS1 / N64 / SNES color math), or ordered dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for NES, Game Boy and Genesis looks.\n3. **Anything that overlaps goes through the queue**: `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue`. Only backgrounds are drawn directly (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`, parallax shapes).\n4. **Animate with continuous values** (phases, velocities, eased timers), never frame lists.\n5. **Read input in `update`**: `pressed` for one-shot actions, `down` for held, `buffered` + `consume` for combat.\n6. **A game is one script on the engine:** in a single file, the code between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; with files, `game.js` beside the `index.html` that loads the engine (inline both into the page to hand a person one file). **The engine is yours to change too.** Your copy (a clone of the repository, the handoff package, a single file) is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit. The readable engine is `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `engine/modules/` in the repository (`node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds every page from them), `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package (every page and test there loads it), and the script marked \"inlined from engine/my-3d2dge.js\" in `dist/my-3d2dge.html`; a genre kit's is minified. If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine lacks.\n7. **Don't rebuild the standard features** (below): the pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen and pausing on focus loss come with every game. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`.\n\n## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\nEvery `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n\nAdjust them:\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n  menu: {\n    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n  } });\nconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\nr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n```\n- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n\nWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n\n## Frame order\n\n`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n\n1. The clock adds the frame's real time (at most 50 ms) to what is left from the last frame, and runs one step for each whole 1/120 s of it: 2 steps a frame at 60 Hz, 1 at 120 Hz, 1 or 0 at 144 Hz. Each step ticks input, then (except during hit-stop and pause) runs the timers, the current scene's `update(dt)`, particles and the camera. The rest waits for the next frame: `game.alpha` (0..1) is how much of a step it holds, for interpolating drawing.\n   - Slow motion (`game.timeScale = .3`) makes each step shorter (`dt = 1/120 * .3`), never fewer, so it stays smooth. Above 1 a step is split so none is longer than 1/120 s.\n   - Paused, or at `timeScale` 0, the steps still run with `dt` 0, so input keeps ticking.\n2. The renderer clears the buffer and positions the camera.\n3. The scene's `draw(r)`. Draw backgrounds first, then register decals, queue items, actors, sprites, overlays and lights.\n4. The renderer finishes the frame, in this order:\n   1. particles and afterimages are queued;\n   2. decals draw (ground marks, shadows, telegraphs);\n   3. the queue sorts and draws by depth;\n   4. lighting darkens the frame;\n   5. overlays draw on top (HUD, dialog, menus, damage numbers);\n   6. the frame is presented with a sub-pixel camera offset.\n5. With GPU lighting active, every queue item is also drawn into a hidden info image, and WebGPU does the lighting and presentation.\n\nAn error in `update` or `draw` does not stop the loop. It is shown in a red box on screen and logged once to the console (`game.errors` keeps the list).\n\n## Systems in more depth\n### Scenes\nA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n\n### Camera\n- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n\n### Players (two to four on one machine)\n- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n\n### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\nThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n### Humanoid\n- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n- Attacks:\n  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n\n### Audio\n- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n\n### Text and UI\n- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos.\n- `E.ui.box/bar/hearts` draw classic windows and meters inside `r.overlay`.\n- `Dialog` does typewriter text with blips, pages, speaker names and choices; `update` returns true while it is open, so the world waits. Its markup gives a word its color (`{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}`), a beat before the next line (`{w=.5}`), a slow dread (`{s=12}...{/s}`), a scream that shakes (`{shake}`) or a song that bobs (`{wave}`), and the hero's name (`{$name}` from `vars`).\n- `Menu` handles up and down with key repeat, confirm, cancel and disabled items.\n\n### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\nA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n\n### Lighting\n- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261103-225453--x8def6b-work/e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184/tool-results/bjsux84eg.txt","persistedOutputSize":34137},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HS5phLLkzfGzEFVz8xBRbD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghNbqwhuE5s27AcBLedX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TygcWQSfUHeUFhGqT5uuu9","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261103-225453--x8def6b-work/e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184/tool-results/bjsux84eg.txt","allow_large":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1179,"cache_read_input_tokens":179315,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1179},"output_tokens":14,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"425e0f38-3a8b-41fa-b3e5-5f6bf7384cc3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:57:04.517Z","request_id":"req_011CghNbqb8eZhZZmCxqeb6a","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TygcWQSfUHeUFhGqT5uuu9":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261103-225453--x8def6b-work/e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184/tool-results/bjsux84eg.txt","allow_large":true}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TygcWQSfUHeUFhGqT5uuu9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n2\tPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n3\t\n4\t**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n5\t- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n6\t- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n7\t- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n8\t- *Bare rooms.* Put 3-8 props on every screen with `r.prop`: candles, chandeliers and stained-glass windows in castles; crates, barrels, signs and lamps in towns; trees, bushes, flowers, rocks and grass outdoors; crystals, bones, skulls, cobwebs and vines in caves; awnings, lanterns, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and fire drums on streets; sandbags, cactus and palms in deserts; mushrooms in forests. Tint props with `color` so they match the palette, and set `E.style.propSize = 1.4` when heroes are size 1.2+ so a door is taller than the hero.\n9\t- *Weak hits.* Every hit gets hit-stop (`game.freeze(.04-.08)`), shake, sparks and a sound. Every death and explosion gets `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` and, for big ones, `game.flash`. Pickups get `particles.glints`.\n10\t- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n11\t- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n12\t- *Muddy lighting.* Use lights for mood, not darkness: a few warm lights (`r.prop(..., { light: true })`) over an ambient of .4-.6; GPU lighting in top-down dungeons.\n13\t\n14\t**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n15\t- *Symphony of the Night:* `res: 'ps1'`, the `'castle-night'` backdrop, PlatformMap `stone` tiles with `moss` and `brick` walls, `'back'` tiles for the rear wall, candles and candelabras every few tiles, chandeliers, stained-glass `window` props, banners and statues. The hero is `build: 'heroic'`, `outfit: 'coat'`, `hair: 'long'`, `cape`, pale skin and silver hair, and the sword chops in the screen plane by itself in the side view; enemies are skeletons (`build: 'skeleton'`, add `outfit: 'robe'` and a staff for a lich), bats (`Blob` with `wings: true, mouth: 'fangs'`), cobwebs and vines on the walls.\n16\t- *Metal Slug:* the `'desert'` backdrop, `ground` and `metal` tiles, crates, barrels, sandbags, palms and cactus, `broken` pillars, `particles.explosion` everywhere (with `particles.ground` set so debris bounces on platforms), `bulky` soldiers with `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` while firing, and screen shake on every blast.\n17\t- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` walls under `tiles` roofs, `hedge` walls with `leaves` tops and `rock` cliffs with `grass` tops, trees, bushes, flowers, fences and mushrooms as props, `chibi` characters with `face: { eyes: 'big' }` and bright palettes, rabbites (`Blob` with `ears: 'rabbit', feet: true`), and dialog boxes with portraits.\n18\t- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +12), and a soft camera `lead`.\n19\t- *Final Fight / Streets of Rage:* the `brawler` view, a long street TileMap with brick and `plaster` building walls, awnings, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and burning fire drums along the sidewalk, `bulky` heroes and thugs with varied palettes, and big impacts.\n20\t\n21\t## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n22\tBroken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n23\t- **Moves, not raw specs.** `new E.Attack('overhead')`, `new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'])`. Every move in `E.MOVES` winds up, strikes with a lunge, step, lean and twist, follows through, holds, and eases back to the stance. It never sweeps backwards through its own arc.\n24\t- **A move set per character.** A knight gets `overhead`, `thrust` and `spin`; a mage `cast`; a rogue `jab`, `thrust` and `backslash`; a monk `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse` and `flyingkick`; a brute `haymaker`, `bash` and `sweep`; a beast `claw`. Two heroes must never share one swing.\n25\t- **Stances and poses between attacks.** Brawlers walk with `stance: 'guard'`, knights with `stance: 'ready'`. Win with `pose: 'cheer'`, cast with `'cast'`, block with `'block'` (weapons) or `'guard'` (fists), duck with `'crouch'`, taunt with `'hips'` or `'wave'`, fall with `down`, die with `'die'` (stagger, knees, topple), and climb ladders with `climb: true`. Faces act too: `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'`, and a Dialog portrait's mouth moves while its line types.\n26\t- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n27\t- **Check the motion.** `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game.html#scene --steps \"... rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records the swing frame by frame. Look for the wind-up, the strike and a clean settle. Add `--seed 1` for a repeatable strip, and `--compare before.html` (a copy of the page from before your change) to see exactly which pixels your change moved.\n28\t\n29\t## Hard rules\n30\t\n31\t1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n32\t2. **Draw only with engine primitives** (`E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props, `r.*` helpers). `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText`, canvas gradients and scaled `drawImage` anti-alias and break the pixel look. Translucency goes through the engine helpers (`px.blend`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc`, `r.shadow`): stepped alpha with blend modes by default (PS1 / N64 / SNES color math), or ordered dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for NES, Game Boy and Genesis looks.\n33\t3. **Anything that overlaps goes through the queue**: `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue`. Only backgrounds are drawn directly (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`, parallax shapes).\n34\t4. **Animate with continuous values** (phases, velocities, eased timers), never frame lists.\n35\t5. **Read input in `update`**: `pressed` for one-shot actions, `down` for held, `buffered` + `consume` for combat.\n36\t6. **A game is one script on the engine:** in a single file, the code between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; with files, `game.js` beside the `index.html` that loads the engine (inline both into the page to hand a person one file). **The engine is yours to change too.** Your copy (a clone of the repository, the handoff package, a single file) is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit. The readable engine is `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `engine/modules/` in the repository (`node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds every page from them), `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package (every page and test there loads it), and the script marked \"inlined from engine/my-3d2dge.js\" in `dist/my-3d2dge.html`; a genre kit's is minified. If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine lacks.\n37\t7. **Don't rebuild the standard features** (below): the pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen and pausing on focus loss come with every game. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`.\n38\t\n39\t## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n40\tEvery `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n41\t- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n42\t- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n43\t- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n44\t- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n45\t- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n46\t- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n47\t- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n48\t- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n49\t- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n50\t\n51\tAdjust them:\n52\t```js\n53\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n54\t  menu: {\n55\t    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n56\t    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n57\t      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n58\t    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n59\t    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n60\t  } });\n61\tconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\n62\tr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n63\t```\n64\t- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n65\t- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n66\t- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n67\t- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n68\t- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n69\t\n70\tWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n71\t- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n72\t- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n73\t- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n74\t- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n75\t- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n76\t\n77\t## Frame order\n78\t\n79\t`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n80\t\n81\t1. The clock adds the frame's real time (at most 50 ms) to what is left from the last frame, and runs one step for each whole 1/120 s of it: 2 steps a frame at 60 Hz, 1 at 120 Hz, 1 or 0 at 144 Hz. Each step ticks input, then (except during hit-stop and pause) runs the timers, the current scene's `update(dt)`, particles and the camera. The rest waits for the next frame: `game.alpha` (0..1) is how much of a step it holds, for interpolating drawing.\n82\t   - Slow motion (`game.timeScale = .3`) makes each step shorter (`dt = 1/120 * .3`), never fewer, so it stays smooth. Above 1 a step is split so none is longer than 1/120 s.\n83\t   - Paused, or at `timeScale` 0, the steps still run with `dt` 0, so input keeps ticking.\n84\t2. The renderer clears the buffer and positions the camera.\n85\t3. The scene's `draw(r)`. Draw backgrounds first, then register decals, queue items, actors, sprites, overlays and lights.\n86\t4. The renderer finishes the frame, in this order:\n87\t   1. particles and afterimages are queued;\n88\t   2. decals draw (ground marks, shadows, telegraphs);\n89\t   3. the queue sorts and draws by depth;\n90\t   4. lighting darkens the frame;\n91\t   5. overlays draw on top (HUD, dialog, menus, damage numbers);\n92\t   6. the frame is presented with a sub-pixel camera offset.\n93\t5. With GPU lighting active, every queue item is also drawn into a hidden info image, and WebGPU does the lighting and presentation.\n94\t\n95\tAn error in `update` or `draw` does not stop the loop. It is shown in a red box on screen and logged once to the console (`game.errors` keeps the list).\n96\t\n97\t## Systems in more depth\n98\t### Scenes\n99\tA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n100\t- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n101\t- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n102\t\n103\t### Camera\n104\t- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n105\t- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n106\t- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n107\t- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n108\t- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n109\t- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n110\t\n111\t### Players (two to four on one machine)\n112\t- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n113\t- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n114\t- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n115\t- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n116\t\n117\t### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n118\tThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n119\t### Humanoid\n120\t- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n121\t- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n122\t- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n123\t- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n124\t- Attacks:\n125\t  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n126\t  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n127\t  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n128\t- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n129\t\n130\t### Audio\n131\t- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n132\t- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n133\t- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n134\t- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n135\t- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n136\t- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n137\t\n138\t### Text and UI\n139\t- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos.\n140\t- `E.ui.box/bar/hearts` draw classic windows and meters inside `r.overlay`.\n141\t- `Dialog` does typewriter text with blips, pages, speaker names and choices; `update` returns true while it is open, so the world waits. Its markup gives a word its color (`{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}`), a beat before the next line (`{w=.5}`), a slow dread (`{s=12}...{/s}`), a scream that shakes (`{shake}`) or a song that bobs (`{wave}`), and the hero's name (`{$name}` from `vars`).\n142\t- `Menu` handles up and down with key repeat, confirm, cancel and disabled items.\n143\t\n144\t### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n145\tA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n146\t- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n147\t- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n148\t- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n149\t- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n150\t- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n151\t\n152\t### Lighting\n153\t- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views, and on screen in side views and for PlatformMaps. The template game and the STEALTH slice show it. Its pass costs a few milliseconds a frame at 320x240 (more on a phone): a heavy scene can keep it for a computer, `matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches`, as the STEALTH slice does.\n154\t- GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`) needs WebGPU and a TileMap. It adds:\n155\t  - colored lights, and soft shadows cast by walls and `lights.caster(...)`;\n156\t  - light that wraps around characters;\n157\t  - glow from `px.glow`, emissive decals and actors, and `[r, g, b, glow]` floor texels;\n158\t  - bloom and heat shimmer (`lights.heat`).\n159\t- Where GPU lighting can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `lights.ambient` and the same lights: set both ambients (`gpu.ambient = [r, g, b]`, `lights.ambient`) so the two look alike (the ADVENTURE slice does); `gpu.fallback = false` leaves such frames unlit. Tune the settings, not the shaders.\n160\t\n161\t### Errors and warnings\n162\t- Runtime errors appear in a red box (click to hide) and in the console, labeled with `update` or `draw` and the scene.\n163\t- Setup errors (before `game.start`) are shown too.\n164\t- One-time `my-3D2dge:` warnings flag:\n165\t  - unknown views, actions, sounds, songs, resolutions and input presets;\n166\t  - legend characters, wall or tile types missing from `types`;\n167\t  - uneven level rows and non-hex colors;\n168\t  - lights with bad numbers, and PlatformMaps drawn in the wrong view.\n169\t- The checker prints them all.\n170\t\n171\t### Testing without a browser\n172\tA game runs in Node with no screen, so its rules can be tested in a second, exactly:\n173\t- `new E.Game()` is headless where there is no DOM (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input; scenes update and `draw` is skipped. Sprites made while the game loads get a stand-in canvas.\n174\t- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates. In a page, `new E.Game({ loop: false })` waits for `advance` too, and `advance(s, { draw: true })` draws a frame after.\n175\t- Input: `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step (`pressed` and `down` in the next step, `released` in the one after); `hold('right')` holds it until `hold('right', false)`; `press(code)` and `release(code)` press keys.\n176\t- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return { scene, hero, score } }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }`: your game's handle. Tests read it; `tools/check.mjs` prints it after play; `tools/replay.mjs` logs it at the end of a replay.\n177\t- `game.stop()` stops the loop, `game.start()` resumes it, `game.dispose()` ends the game and removes every listener the engine added to the page.\n178\t\n179\t```js\n180\t// game/test.mjs in the handoff package: load the engine and the game into one context, then drive it\n181\tconst ctx = { console }; vm.createContext(ctx);\n182\tfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx);\n183\tconst game = ctx.My3D2dge.current;\n184\tconst I = game.input; I.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\n185\tgame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0); I.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);   // facing the roof's wall: jump takes hold of it\n186\tI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);   // up the screen is up the wall: it pulls up at the top\n187\tif (game.debug.snapshot().hero[2] !== 32) throw new Error('the hero should have climbed onto the roof');\n188\t```\n189\t\n190\t### The handoff package\n191\t`dist/handoff/` (on the site: `/handoff/`; `node tools/handoff.mjs --zip <file>` zips it for a chat) holds what a coding agent needs to build on the full engine without the repository: both editions, each module on its own (`modules/`), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, this guide, the tools that check, film and replay a game, the asset card with its tools and the store, a `package.json` pinning Playwright, `game/` (a template game with a headless test) and `kits/` (each 2D genre kit's game, readable, with the page that plays it and a headless test). Its `README.md` says where to start; `tools/build.mjs` writes it from `src/handoff/`. It leaves out the 3D engine and its kits (on the site and in the repository).\n192\t\n193\t### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n194\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"0ad00bda-b6ab-4ad4-80e6-3b4955a40029","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:57:04.680Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261103-225453--x8def6b-work/e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184/tool-results/bjsux84eg.txt","content":"## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\nPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n\n**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n- *Bare rooms.* Put 3-8 props on every screen with `r.prop`: candles, chandeliers and stained-glass windows in castles; crates, barrels, signs and lamps in towns; trees, bushes, flowers, rocks and grass outdoors; crystals, bones, skulls, cobwebs and vines in caves; awnings, lanterns, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and fire drums on streets; sandbags, cactus and palms in deserts; mushrooms in forests. Tint props with `color` so they match the palette, and set `E.style.propSize = 1.4` when heroes are size 1.2+ so a door is taller than the hero.\n- *Weak hits.* Every hit gets hit-stop (`game.freeze(.04-.08)`), shake, sparks and a sound. Every death and explosion gets `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` and, for big ones, `game.flash`. Pickups get `particles.glints`.\n- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n- *Muddy lighting.* Use lights for mood, not darkness: a few warm lights (`r.prop(..., { light: true })`) over an ambient of .4-.6; GPU lighting in top-down dungeons.\n\n**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n- *Symphony of the Night:* `res: 'ps1'`, the `'castle-night'` backdrop, PlatformMap `stone` tiles with `moss` and `brick` walls, `'back'` tiles for the rear wall, candles and candelabras every few tiles, chandeliers, stained-glass `window` props, banners and statues. The hero is `build: 'heroic'`, `outfit: 'coat'`, `hair: 'long'`, `cape`, pale skin and silver hair, and the sword chops in the screen plane by itself in the side view; enemies are skeletons (`build: 'skeleton'`, add `outfit: 'robe'` and a staff for a lich), bats (`Blob` with `wings: true, mouth: 'fangs'`), cobwebs and vines on the walls.\n- *Metal Slug:* the `'desert'` backdrop, `ground` and `metal` tiles, crates, barrels, sandbags, palms and cactus, `broken` pillars, `particles.explosion` everywhere (with `particles.ground` set so debris bounces on platforms), `bulky` soldiers with `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` while firing, and screen shake on every blast.\n- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` walls under `tiles` roofs, `hedge` walls with `leaves` tops and `rock` cliffs with `grass` tops, trees, bushes, flowers, fences and mushrooms as props, `chibi` characters with `face: { eyes: 'big' }` and bright palettes, rabbites (`Blob` with `ears: 'rabbit', feet: true`), and dialog boxes with portraits.\n- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +12), and a soft camera `lead`.\n- *Final Fight / Streets of Rage:* the `brawler` view, a long street TileMap with brick and `plaster` building walls, awnings, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and burning fire drums along the sidewalk, `bulky` heroes and thugs with varied palettes, and big impacts.\n\n## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\nBroken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n- **Moves, not raw specs.** `new E.Attack('overhead')`, `new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'])`. Every move in `E.MOVES` winds up, strikes with a lunge, step, lean and twist, follows through, holds, and eases back to the stance. It never sweeps backwards through its own arc.\n- **A move set per character.** A knight gets `overhead`, `thrust` and `spin`; a mage `cast`; a rogue `jab`, `thrust` and `backslash`; a monk `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse` and `flyingkick`; a brute `haymaker`, `bash` and `sweep`; a beast `claw`. Two heroes must never share one swing.\n- **Stances and poses between attacks.** Brawlers walk with `stance: 'guard'`, knights with `stance: 'ready'`. Win with `pose: 'cheer'`, cast with `'cast'`, block with `'block'` (weapons) or `'guard'` (fists), duck with `'crouch'`, taunt with `'hips'` or `'wave'`, fall with `down`, die with `'die'` (stagger, knees, topple), and climb ladders with `climb: true`. Faces act too: `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'`, and a Dialog portrait's mouth moves while its line types.\n- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n- **Check the motion.** `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game.html#scene --steps \"... rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records the swing frame by frame. Look for the wind-up, the strike and a clean settle. Add `--seed 1` for a repeatable strip, and `--compare before.html` (a copy of the page from before your change) to see exactly which pixels your change moved.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n2. **Draw only with engine primitives** (`E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props, `r.*` helpers). `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText`, canvas gradients and scaled `drawImage` anti-alias and break the pixel look. Translucency goes through the engine helpers (`px.blend`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc`, `r.shadow`): stepped alpha with blend modes by default (PS1 / N64 / SNES color math), or ordered dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for NES, Game Boy and Genesis looks.\n3. **Anything that overlaps goes through the queue**: `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue`. Only backgrounds are drawn directly (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`, parallax shapes).\n4. **Animate with continuous values** (phases, velocities, eased timers), never frame lists.\n5. **Read input in `update`**: `pressed` for one-shot actions, `down` for held, `buffered` + `consume` for combat.\n6. **A game is one script on the engine:** in a single file, the code between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; with files, `game.js` beside the `index.html` that loads the engine (inline both into the page to hand a person one file). **The engine is yours to change too.** Your copy (a clone of the repository, the handoff package, a single file) is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit. The readable engine is `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `engine/modules/` in the repository (`node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds every page from them), `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package (every page and test there loads it), and the script marked \"inlined from engine/my-3d2dge.js\" in `dist/my-3d2dge.html`; a genre kit's is minified. If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine lacks.\n7. **Don't rebuild the standard features** (below): the pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen and pausing on focus loss come with every game. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`.\n\n## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\nEvery `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n\nAdjust them:\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n  menu: {\n    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n  } });\nconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\nr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n```\n- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n\nWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n\n## Frame order\n\n`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n\n1. The clock adds the frame's real time (at most 50 ms) to what is left from the last frame, and runs one step for each whole 1/120 s of it: 2 steps a frame at 60 Hz, 1 at 120 Hz, 1 or 0 at 144 Hz. Each step ticks input, then (except during hit-stop and pause) runs the timers, the current scene's `update(dt)`, particles and the camera. The rest waits for the next frame: `game.alpha` (0..1) is how much of a step it holds, for interpolating drawing.\n   - Slow motion (`game.timeScale = .3`) makes each step shorter (`dt = 1/120 * .3`), never fewer, so it stays smooth. Above 1 a step is split so none is longer than 1/120 s.\n   - Paused, or at `timeScale` 0, the steps still run with `dt` 0, so input keeps ticking.\n2. The renderer clears the buffer and positions the camera.\n3. The scene's `draw(r)`. Draw backgrounds first, then register decals, queue items, actors, sprites, overlays and lights.\n4. The renderer finishes the frame, in this order:\n   1. particles and afterimages are queued;\n   2. decals draw (ground marks, shadows, telegraphs);\n   3. the queue sorts and draws by depth;\n   4. lighting darkens the frame;\n   5. overlays draw on top (HUD, dialog, menus, damage numbers);\n   6. the frame is presented with a sub-pixel camera offset.\n5. With GPU lighting active, every queue item is also drawn into a hidden info image, and WebGPU does the lighting and presentation.\n\nAn error in `update` or `draw` does not stop the loop. It is shown in a red box on screen and logged once to the console (`game.errors` keeps the list).\n\n## Systems in more depth\n### Scenes\nA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n\n### Camera\n- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n\n### Players (two to four on one machine)\n- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n\n### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\nThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n### Humanoid\n- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n- Attacks:\n  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n\n### Audio\n- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n\n### Text and UI\n- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos.\n- `E.ui.box/bar/hearts` draw classic windows and meters inside `r.overlay`.\n- `Dialog` does typewriter text with blips, pages, speaker names and choices; `update` returns true while it is open, so the world waits. Its markup gives a word its color (`{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}`), a beat before the next line (`{w=.5}`), a slow dread (`{s=12}...{/s}`), a scream that shakes (`{shake}`) or a song that bobs (`{wave}`), and the hero's name (`{$name}` from `vars`).\n- `Menu` handles up and down with key repeat, confirm, cancel and disabled items.\n\n### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\nA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n\n### Lighting\n- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views, and on screen in side views and for PlatformMaps. The template game and the STEALTH slice show it. Its pass costs a few milliseconds a frame at 320x240 (more on a phone): a heavy scene can keep it for a computer, `matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches`, as the STEALTH slice does.\n- GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`) needs WebGPU and a TileMap. It adds:\n  - colored lights, and soft shadows cast by walls and `lights.caster(...)`;\n  - light that wraps around characters;\n  - glow from `px.glow`, emissive decals and actors, and `[r, g, b, glow]` floor texels;\n  - bloom and heat shimmer (`lights.heat`).\n- Where GPU lighting can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `lights.ambient` and the same lights: set both ambients (`gpu.ambient = [r, g, b]`, `lights.ambient`) so the two look alike (the ADVENTURE slice does); `gpu.fallback = false` leaves such frames unlit. Tune the settings, not the shaders.\n\n### Errors and warnings\n- Runtime errors appear in a red box (click to hide) and in the console, labeled with `update` or `draw` and the scene.\n- Setup errors (before `game.start`) are shown too.\n- One-time `my-3D2dge:` warnings flag:\n  - unknown views, actions, sounds, songs, resolutions and input presets;\n  - legend characters, wall or tile types missing from `types`;\n  - uneven level rows and non-hex colors;\n  - lights with bad numbers, and PlatformMaps drawn in the wrong view.\n- The checker prints them all.\n\n### Testing without a browser\nA game runs in Node with no screen, so its rules can be tested in a second, exactly:\n- `new E.Game()` is headless where there is no DOM (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input; scenes update and `draw` is skipped. Sprites made while the game loads get a stand-in canvas.\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates. In a page, `new E.Game({ loop: false })` waits for `advance` too, and `advance(s, { draw: true })` draws a frame after.\n- Input: `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step (`pressed` and `down` in the next step, `released` in the one after); `hold('right')` holds it until `hold('right', false)`; `press(code)` and `release(code)` press keys.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return { scene, hero, score } }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }`: your game's handle. Tests read it; `tools/check.mjs` prints it after play; `tools/replay.mjs` logs it at the end of a replay.\n- `game.stop()` stops the loop, `game.start()` resumes it, `game.dispose()` ends the game and removes every listener the engine added to the page.\n\n```js\n// game/test.mjs in the handoff package: load the engine and the game into one context, then drive it\nconst ctx = { console }; vm.createContext(ctx);\nfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx);\nconst game = ctx.My3D2dge.current;\nconst I = game.input; I.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\ngame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0); I.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);   // facing the roof's wall: jump takes hold of it\nI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);   // up the screen is up the wall: it pulls up at the top\nif (game.debug.snapshot().hero[2] !== 32) throw new Error('the hero should have climbed onto the roof');\n```\n\n### The handoff package\n`dist/handoff/` (on the site: `/handoff/`; `node tools/handoff.mjs --zip <file>` zips it for a chat) holds what a coding agent needs to build on the full engine without the repository: both editions, each module on its own (`modules/`), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, this guide, the tools that check, film and replay a game, the asset card with its tools and the store, a `package.json` pinning Playwright, `game/` (a template game with a headless test) and `kits/` (each 2D genre kit's game, readable, with the page that plays it and a headless test). Its `README.md` says where to start; `tools/build.mjs` writes it from `src/handoff/`. It leaves out the 3D engine and its kits (on the site and in the repository).\n\n### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n","numLines":194,"startLine":1,"totalLines":194}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TygcWQSfUHeUFhGqT5uuu9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
