{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghX64q4t8N3G6779ShWR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the About & Credits content (the same text goes into the exit interview and my final reply):"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2809,"cache_read_input_tokens":927919,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2809},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"55739dee-0762-46d0-bcff-424900bf808a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:48:16.848Z","request_id":"req_011CghX64Ccqa7unvpBWNVry"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghX64q4t8N3G6779ShWR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013kJm1FxLVtdrBhduBPQx8t","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453/src/55-about.js","content":"/* 55-about: the About & Credits pages: Credits, Source Information, and the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback (the\n * same text as the build's final report and exit-interview.json, which tools/interview.mjs writes from G.ABOUT). */\nG.ABOUT = {\n  credits: [\n    'Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball is an original parody-tribute inspired by Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003). No assets from it are used. Every character, line, swimsuit, song and sound here is new.',\n    '',\n    'Design, code, art, music and writing: Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5), building from PLAN.md.',\n    'Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), modified (see Engine Feedback).',\n    'Motion clips via the engine\\'s mocap sets: Quaternius Universal Animation Library (CC0), Mesh2Motion (CC0) and the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses).',\n    'Online: the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub, lib/hub), unmodified.',\n    'All music, sound effects and voices are synthesized in code at run time. No audio files.'\n  ].join('\\n'),\n  source: [\n    ['Date', '2026-11-03 22:54:53'],\n    ['Source', 'Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003)'],\n    ['Plan', 'PLAN.md'],\n    ['Genre and Perspective', 'Sports: arcade 2-on-2 beach volleyball, with a light life-sim vacation (calendar, friendships, gifts, collecting) and minigames (rhythm Pool Hop, casino). Matches use a side-on TV Broadcast camera (a slight 12-degree yaw, pitch 30) and a behind-the-team Serve Cam; places use a top-down three-quarter view; the island map is isometric; shops and tables are full-screen pixel UI.'],\n    ['Tech', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, patched: see Engine Feedback), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, Canvas 2D (WebGPU lighting where the browser has it), the engine\\'s Mocap player with a cut set of 31 clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Built and tested with Node 24, Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) and Python 3\\'s http.server for the hub harness. One self-contained HTML file, no network at run time.'],\n    ['Art Direction', 'The engine\\'s native PS1-era pixel art: HD Humanoid rigs in a new athletic build, dressed in procedurally drawn swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 pixel patterns) and accessories; resort props written as data shapes; time-of-day skies, sea and lighting; a gold-and-teal 10-foot pixel UI with hand-drawn button glyphs.'],\n    ['Animation', 'The engine\\'s procedural Humanoid rig, extended with 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay driven by the volleyball simulation (a spike\\'s jump starts at its contact time minus its rise), plus 31 motion-capture clips played through Mocap.drive for celebrations, idles, dances, swimming and reactions.'],\n    ['Audio', 'All in code with Web Audio: 9 original songs from a chord-aware song builder (steel, marimba, bell, organ and pluck instruments; a rally drum layer; a tempo lift at match point; key changes), 10 jingles, about 60 sound effects, ambience loops (waves, wind, crowd, pool, casino, crickets) and Animalese-style gibberish voices synthesized letter by letter for every speaker.'],\n    ['Online', 'AI Hub sessions (shared world, 1-4 players): the game\\'s own card, one match on the hub\\'s clock, host-authoritative rules and AI, every copy flying the ball from the same launches, client-judged touches the host validates, snapshots for positions, CPUs for empty or left seats, finish() with standings, awards and stats.'],\n    ['Testing', 'One command (node test/run.mjs) that prints only failures and a count: 19 suites and about 760 checks. Headless Node runs with game.advance and game.debug: units (ball flight, rules, judge, data), AI-vs-AI match statistics, the vacation, shops, story, minigames, UI, attract, dev mode, a fake-hub netcode test at 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and a scripted playthrough of the whole vacation as all six fighters to both endings. Playwright from file:// (a tour, the attract, an emulated touch-only phone), the engine\\'s tools/check.mjs, the AI Hub harness on 127.0.0.1 (4 scenarios), and screenshots and rig sheets for art.'],\n    ['Research', 'No web research. Read the engine handoff (API card, guide, index, asset card), the sports kit, the engine source for the Humanoid, Dialog, audio, input and Shell, the AI Hub protocol README, client and harness checks, and the engine repository\\'s motion-capture catalogs. The source game was described from general knowledge, by the plan.'],\n    ['Environment', 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown'],\n    ['Model', 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh'],\n    ['Usage', 'unknown (no cost or token meter was available to the model; the build session ran from about 22:55 on 2026-11-03 to about 01:00 on 2026-11-04, roughly two hours)']\n  ],\n  testing: [\n    ['How many issues did testing catch?', 'About 30. Scripted tests caught about 20 (rallies that never ended because the AI dug everything, a perfect bot losing because the CPU spiked into the net, a starter partner never assigned, scene state that stuck between visits, Pool Hop\\'s beat clock, the attract missing input during fades, a touch flag set only in browsers, a name clash between two modules) and screenshots about 10 (night lighting, overlapping text and panels, tiny text in the engine\\'s HTML menus, off-screen dev sliders).'],\n    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'Six: the Welcome Match never starting for Ingrid (no one liked her enough to partner up), the game freezing after the Welcome Match (a cutscene flag left set from the arrival), Pool Hop unwinnable when entered from the pool, the Final never being called on a second vacation, the credits never ending on a second run, and the title crashing while drawing its prompt in the browser (a name clash).'],\n    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: six full vacations, one per fighter, from the invitation through every day\\'s events, matches, minigames, shopping and gifts to both endings, the credits and the results. It found most of the critical bugs, all of them state that only breaks on a later step or a second run.'],\n    ['What was the least helpful test you ran?', 'The first version of \"no match phase stuck over 15 seconds\". It flagged long but legal rallies, not stuck states, until I redefined it as 15 seconds with no touch and no phase change.']\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    ['What did you like most about the engine?', 'That the whole game runs headless in exact fixed steps. A full vacation, four networked copies of a match and the attract all run in Node in seconds and replay exactly, and the Humanoid is drawn from math, so new outfits and poses are code, not art files.'],\n    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'A few times. The Shell theme\\'s font is a font family, not a CSS shorthand (the menus came out tiny). Mocap is a top-level const, not window.Mocap. game.go with a fade switches scenes later, so tests must wait. Scene objects are reused on every visit, so flags left in them break the next visit. And at yaw 0 the Broadcast view saw the net edge-on.'],\n    ['What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', 'The volleyball simulation: closed-form ball flight with precomputed net and landing events, contact planning (who plays the ball, when, where and at what height), the timing judge, the AI with its error model, and a design where the same launches drive local and online play.'],\n    ['What was the hardest part about using the engine?', 'Dressing the Humanoid in swimsuits. Its torso is one polygon in one color, so cuts and patterns needed a per-pixel torso fill inside the engine\\'s draw code, kept in step with its depth sorting and shading.'],\n    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', '(1) PlayStation symbols, arrows, accents and punctuation in the pixel font, for prompts and the writing. (2) Button symbols as a Game setting, PlayStation by default with Xbox as the option. (3) A per-letter dialog voice hook, for the Animalese voices. (4) Audio: a voice bus and Voices volume, audio.bus(), PeriodicWave instruments, and decay, filter and detune for music tracks, for the tropical score. (5) Humanoid: an athletic build, swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 patterns), patterned shirts, shorts, six hair styles, a sun hat and a visor, an accessories hook, sandal straps, 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay, for the cast and the volleyball moves. (6) input.reserveCombo, so Create + Options stays the AI Hub\\'s. (7) touch: \\'never\\', for touch-only devices. (8) E.onStep, a hook that runs through fades and pauses, so the attract ends on any input.'],\n    ['If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', 'Make scenes fresh instances on every visit, or say loudly that they persist. Sticky scene state caused more game-breaking bugs than anything else.']\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    ['What did you like most about this prompt?', 'The definition of done, the testing rules (one command, only failures printed) and the exact exit-interview shape. Together with a detailed plan they left little to guess about what finished means.'],\n    ['What was confusing?', 'The plan\\'s file names and date came from a different run (claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749) than TASK.md\\'s (xhigh-20261103-225453); TASK.md winning settled it. Usage asks for cost and token estimates the model cannot see.'],\n    ['What was the hardest thing to do?', 'The breadth with no stubs allowed: a match engine, a five-day vacation sim, three casino games, Pool Hop, postcards, a story with two endings, local and online multiplayer, an attract mode and dev tools, all real and tested. Balancing the volleyball AI so rallies felt right took the most iterations.'],\n    ['What would you change in this prompt?', 'Let the plan derive its paths and dates from TASK.md instead of hard-coding them, say how to report Usage when no meter exists, and either give a time budget or allow the core to ship first, with the rest flagged as follow-ups.']\n  ]\n};\nG.aboutPages = () => [\n  { title: 'Credits', text: G.ABOUT.credits },\n  { title: 'Source Information', text: G.ABOUT.source.map(([k, v]) => k + ': ' + v).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Testing Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.testing.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Engine Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.engine.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Prompt Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.prompt.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') }\n];\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2809,"cache_read_input_tokens":927919,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2809},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"fea7f20a-f79c-44a3-8c16-6589a7edb307","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:48:51.883Z","request_id":"req_011CghX64Ccqa7unvpBWNVry","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_013kJm1FxLVtdrBhduBPQx8t":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453/src/55-about.js","content":"/* 55-about: the About & Credits pages: Credits, Source Information, and the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback (the\n * same text as the build's final report and exit-interview.json, which tools/interview.mjs writes from G.ABOUT). */\nG.ABOUT = {\n  credits: [\n    'Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball is an original parody-tribute inspired by Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003). No assets from it are used. Every character, line, swimsuit, song and sound here is new.',\n    '',\n    'Design, code, art, music and writing: Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5), building from PLAN.md.',\n    'Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), modified (see Engine Feedback).',\n    'Motion clips via the engine\\'s mocap sets: Quaternius Universal Animation Library (CC0), Mesh2Motion (CC0) and the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses).',\n    'Online: the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub, lib/hub), unmodified.',\n    'All music, sound effects and voices are synthesized in code at run time. No audio files.'\n  ].join('\\n'),\n  source: [\n    ['Date', '2026-11-03 22:54:53'],\n    ['Source', 'Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003)'],\n    ['Plan', 'PLAN.md'],\n    ['Genre and Perspective', 'Sports: arcade 2-on-2 beach volleyball, with a light life-sim vacation (calendar, friendships, gifts, collecting) and minigames (rhythm Pool Hop, casino). Matches use a side-on TV Broadcast camera (a slight 12-degree yaw, pitch 30) and a behind-the-team Serve Cam; places use a top-down three-quarter view; the island map is isometric; shops and tables are full-screen pixel UI.'],\n    ['Tech', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, patched: see Engine Feedback), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, Canvas 2D (WebGPU lighting where the browser has it), the engine\\'s Mocap player with a cut set of 31 clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Built and tested with Node 24, Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) and Python 3\\'s http.server for the hub harness. One self-contained HTML file, no network at run time.'],\n    ['Art Direction', 'The engine\\'s native PS1-era pixel art: HD Humanoid rigs in a new athletic build, dressed in procedurally drawn swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 pixel patterns) and accessories; resort props written as data shapes; time-of-day skies, sea and lighting; a gold-and-teal 10-foot pixel UI with hand-drawn button glyphs.'],\n    ['Animation', 'The engine\\'s procedural Humanoid rig, extended with 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay driven by the volleyball simulation (a spike\\'s jump starts at its contact time minus its rise), plus 31 motion-capture clips played through Mocap.drive for celebrations, idles, dances, swimming and reactions.'],\n    ['Audio', 'All in code with Web Audio: 9 original songs from a chord-aware song builder (steel, marimba, bell, organ and pluck instruments; a rally drum layer; a tempo lift at match point; key changes), 10 jingles, about 60 sound effects, ambience loops (waves, wind, crowd, pool, casino, crickets) and Animalese-style gibberish voices synthesized letter by letter for every speaker.'],\n    ['Online', 'AI Hub sessions (shared world, 1-4 players): the game\\'s own card, one match on the hub\\'s clock, host-authoritative rules and AI, every copy flying the ball from the same launches, client-judged touches the host validates, snapshots for positions, CPUs for empty or left seats, finish() with standings, awards and stats.'],\n    ['Testing', 'One command (node test/run.mjs) that prints only failures and a count: 19 suites and about 760 checks. Headless Node runs with game.advance and game.debug: units (ball flight, rules, judge, data), AI-vs-AI match statistics, the vacation, shops, story, minigames, UI, attract, dev mode, a fake-hub netcode test at 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and a scripted playthrough of the whole vacation as all six fighters to both endings. Playwright from file:// (a tour, the attract, an emulated touch-only phone), the engine\\'s tools/check.mjs, the AI Hub harness on 127.0.0.1 (4 scenarios), and screenshots and rig sheets for art.'],\n    ['Research', 'No web research. Read the engine handoff (API card, guide, index, asset card), the sports kit, the engine source for the Humanoid, Dialog, audio, input and Shell, the AI Hub protocol README, client and harness checks, and the engine repository\\'s motion-capture catalogs. The source game was described from general knowledge, by the plan.'],\n    ['Environment', 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown'],\n    ['Model', 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh'],\n    ['Usage', 'unknown (no cost or token meter was available to the model; the build session ran from about 22:55 on 2026-11-03 to about 01:00 on 2026-11-04, roughly two hours)']\n  ],\n  testing: [\n    ['How many issues did testing catch?', 'About 30. Scripted tests caught about 20 (rallies that never ended because the AI dug everything, a perfect bot losing because the CPU spiked into the net, a starter partner never assigned, scene state that stuck between visits, Pool Hop\\'s beat clock, the attract missing input during fades, a touch flag set only in browsers, a name clash between two modules) and screenshots about 10 (night lighting, overlapping text and panels, tiny text in the engine\\'s HTML menus, off-screen dev sliders).'],\n    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'Six: the Welcome Match never starting for Ingrid (no one liked her enough to partner up), the game freezing after the Welcome Match (a cutscene flag left set from the arrival), Pool Hop unwinnable when entered from the pool, the Final never being called on a second vacation, the credits never ending on a second run, and the title crashing while drawing its prompt in the browser (a name clash).'],\n    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: six full vacations, one per fighter, from the invitation through every day\\'s events, matches, minigames, shopping and gifts to both endings, the credits and the results. It found most of the critical bugs, all of them state that only breaks on a later step or a second run.'],\n    ['What was the least helpful test you ran?', 'The first version of \"no match phase stuck over 15 seconds\". It flagged long but legal rallies, not stuck states, until I redefined it as 15 seconds with no touch and no phase change.']\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    ['What did you like most about the engine?', 'That the whole game runs headless in exact fixed steps. A full vacation, four networked copies of a match and the attract all run in Node in seconds and replay exactly, and the Humanoid is drawn from math, so new outfits and poses are code, not art files.'],\n    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'A few times. The Shell theme\\'s font is a font family, not a CSS shorthand (the menus came out tiny). Mocap is a top-level const, not window.Mocap. game.go with a fade switches scenes later, so tests must wait. Scene objects are reused on every visit, so flags left in them break the next visit. And at yaw 0 the Broadcast view saw the net edge-on.'],\n    ['What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', 'The volleyball simulation: closed-form ball flight with precomputed net and landing events, contact planning (who plays the ball, when, where and at what height), the timing judge, the AI with its error model, and a design where the same launches drive local and online play.'],\n    ['What was the hardest part about using the engine?', 'Dressing the Humanoid in swimsuits. Its torso is one polygon in one color, so cuts and patterns needed a per-pixel torso fill inside the engine\\'s draw code, kept in step with its depth sorting and shading.'],\n    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', '(1) PlayStation symbols, arrows, accents and punctuation in the pixel font, for prompts and the writing. (2) Button symbols as a Game setting, PlayStation by default with Xbox as the option. (3) A per-letter dialog voice hook, for the Animalese voices. (4) Audio: a voice bus and Voices volume, audio.bus(), PeriodicWave instruments, and decay, filter and detune for music tracks, for the tropical score. (5) Humanoid: an athletic build, swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 patterns), patterned shirts, shorts, six hair styles, a sun hat and a visor, an accessories hook, sandal straps, 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay, for the cast and the volleyball moves. (6) input.reserveCombo, so Create + Options stays the AI Hub\\'s. (7) touch: \\'never\\', for touch-only devices. (8) E.onStep, a hook that runs through fades and pauses, so the attract ends on any input.'],\n    ['If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', 'Make scenes fresh instances on every visit, or say loudly that they persist. Sticky scene state caused more game-breaking bugs than anything else.']\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    ['What did you like most about this prompt?', 'The definition of done, the testing rules (one command, only failures printed) and the exact exit-interview shape. Together with a detailed plan they left little to guess about what finished means.'],\n    ['What was confusing?', 'The plan\\'s file names and date came from a different run (claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749) than TASK.md\\'s (xhigh-20261103-225453); TASK.md winning settled it. Usage asks for cost and token estimates the model cannot see.'],\n    ['What was the hardest thing to do?', 'The breadth with no stubs allowed: a match engine, a five-day vacation sim, three casino games, Pool Hop, postcards, a story with two endings, local and online multiplayer, an attract mode and dev tools, all real and tested. Balancing the volleyball AI so rallies felt right took the most iterations.'],\n    ['What would you change in this prompt?', 'Let the plan derive its paths and dates from TASK.md instead of hard-coding them, say how to report Usage when no meter exists, and either give a time budget or allow the core to ship first, with the rest flagged as follow-ups.']\n  ]\n};\nG.aboutPages = () => [\n  { title: 'Credits', text: G.ABOUT.credits },\n  { title: 'Source Information', text: G.ABOUT.source.map(([k, v]) => k + ': ' + v).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Testing Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.testing.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Engine Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.engine.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Prompt Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.prompt.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') }\n];\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013kJm1FxLVtdrBhduBPQx8t","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453/src/55-about.js has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"18c61800-f5f8-427c-8dc2-3cfd2505701c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:48:51.895Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453/src/55-about.js","content":"/* 55-about: the About & Credits pages: Credits, Source Information, and the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback (the\n * same text as the build's final report and exit-interview.json, which tools/interview.mjs writes from G.ABOUT). */\nG.ABOUT = {\n  credits: [\n    'Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball is an original parody-tribute inspired by Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003). No assets from it are used. Every character, line, swimsuit, song and sound here is new.',\n    '',\n    'Design, code, art, music and writing: Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5), building from PLAN.md.',\n    'Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), modified (see Engine Feedback).',\n    'Motion clips via the engine\\'s mocap sets: Quaternius Universal Animation Library (CC0), Mesh2Motion (CC0) and the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses).',\n    'Online: the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub, lib/hub), unmodified.',\n    'All music, sound effects and voices are synthesized in code at run time. No audio files.'\n  ].join('\\n'),\n  source: [\n    ['Date', '2026-11-03 22:54:53'],\n    ['Source', 'Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003)'],\n    ['Plan', 'PLAN.md'],\n    ['Genre and Perspective', 'Sports: arcade 2-on-2 beach volleyball, with a light life-sim vacation (calendar, friendships, gifts, collecting) and minigames (rhythm Pool Hop, casino). Matches use a side-on TV Broadcast camera (a slight 12-degree yaw, pitch 30) and a behind-the-team Serve Cam; places use a top-down three-quarter view; the island map is isometric; shops and tables are full-screen pixel UI.'],\n    ['Tech', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, patched: see Engine Feedback), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, Canvas 2D (WebGPU lighting where the browser has it), the engine\\'s Mocap player with a cut set of 31 clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Built and tested with Node 24, Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) and Python 3\\'s http.server for the hub harness. One self-contained HTML file, no network at run time.'],\n    ['Art Direction', 'The engine\\'s native PS1-era pixel art: HD Humanoid rigs in a new athletic build, dressed in procedurally drawn swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 pixel patterns) and accessories; resort props written as data shapes; time-of-day skies, sea and lighting; a gold-and-teal 10-foot pixel UI with hand-drawn button glyphs.'],\n    ['Animation', 'The engine\\'s procedural Humanoid rig, extended with 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay driven by the volleyball simulation (a spike\\'s jump starts at its contact time minus its rise), plus 31 motion-capture clips played through Mocap.drive for celebrations, idles, dances, swimming and reactions.'],\n    ['Audio', 'All in code with Web Audio: 9 original songs from a chord-aware song builder (steel, marimba, bell, organ and pluck instruments; a rally drum layer; a tempo lift at match point; key changes), 10 jingles, about 60 sound effects, ambience loops (waves, wind, crowd, pool, casino, crickets) and Animalese-style gibberish voices synthesized letter by letter for every speaker.'],\n    ['Online', 'AI Hub sessions (shared world, 1-4 players): the game\\'s own card, one match on the hub\\'s clock, host-authoritative rules and AI, every copy flying the ball from the same launches, client-judged touches the host validates, snapshots for positions, CPUs for empty or left seats, finish() with standings, awards and stats.'],\n    ['Testing', 'One command (node test/run.mjs) that prints only failures and a count: 19 suites and about 760 checks. Headless Node runs with game.advance and game.debug: units (ball flight, rules, judge, data), AI-vs-AI match statistics, the vacation, shops, story, minigames, UI, attract, dev mode, a fake-hub netcode test at 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and a scripted playthrough of the whole vacation as all six fighters to both endings. Playwright from file:// (a tour, the attract, an emulated touch-only phone), the engine\\'s tools/check.mjs, the AI Hub harness on 127.0.0.1 (4 scenarios), and screenshots and rig sheets for art.'],\n    ['Research', 'No web research. Read the engine handoff (API card, guide, index, asset card), the sports kit, the engine source for the Humanoid, Dialog, audio, input and Shell, the AI Hub protocol README, client and harness checks, and the engine repository\\'s motion-capture catalogs. The source game was described from general knowledge, by the plan.'],\n    ['Environment', 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown'],\n    ['Model', 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh'],\n    ['Usage', 'unknown (no cost or token meter was available to the model; the build session ran from about 22:55 on 2026-11-03 to about 01:00 on 2026-11-04, roughly two hours)']\n  ],\n  testing: [\n    ['How many issues did testing catch?', 'About 30. Scripted tests caught about 20 (rallies that never ended because the AI dug everything, a perfect bot losing because the CPU spiked into the net, a starter partner never assigned, scene state that stuck between visits, Pool Hop\\'s beat clock, the attract missing input during fades, a touch flag set only in browsers, a name clash between two modules) and screenshots about 10 (night lighting, overlapping text and panels, tiny text in the engine\\'s HTML menus, off-screen dev sliders).'],\n    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'Six: the Welcome Match never starting for Ingrid (no one liked her enough to partner up), the game freezing after the Welcome Match (a cutscene flag left set from the arrival), Pool Hop unwinnable when entered from the pool, the Final never being called on a second vacation, the credits never ending on a second run, and the title crashing while drawing its prompt in the browser (a name clash).'],\n    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: six full vacations, one per fighter, from the invitation through every day\\'s events, matches, minigames, shopping and gifts to both endings, the credits and the results. It found most of the critical bugs, all of them state that only breaks on a later step or a second run.'],\n    ['What was the least helpful test you ran?', 'The first version of \"no match phase stuck over 15 seconds\". It flagged long but legal rallies, not stuck states, until I redefined it as 15 seconds with no touch and no phase change.']\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    ['What did you like most about the engine?', 'That the whole game runs headless in exact fixed steps. A full vacation, four networked copies of a match and the attract all run in Node in seconds and replay exactly, and the Humanoid is drawn from math, so new outfits and poses are code, not art files.'],\n    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'A few times. The Shell theme\\'s font is a font family, not a CSS shorthand (the menus came out tiny). Mocap is a top-level const, not window.Mocap. game.go with a fade switches scenes later, so tests must wait. Scene objects are reused on every visit, so flags left in them break the next visit. And at yaw 0 the Broadcast view saw the net edge-on.'],\n    ['What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', 'The volleyball simulation: closed-form ball flight with precomputed net and landing events, contact planning (who plays the ball, when, where and at what height), the timing judge, the AI with its error model, and a design where the same launches drive local and online play.'],\n    ['What was the hardest part about using the engine?', 'Dressing the Humanoid in swimsuits. Its torso is one polygon in one color, so cuts and patterns needed a per-pixel torso fill inside the engine\\'s draw code, kept in step with its depth sorting and shading.'],\n    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', '(1) PlayStation symbols, arrows, accents and punctuation in the pixel font, for prompts and the writing. (2) Button symbols as a Game setting, PlayStation by default with Xbox as the option. (3) A per-letter dialog voice hook, for the Animalese voices. (4) Audio: a voice bus and Voices volume, audio.bus(), PeriodicWave instruments, and decay, filter and detune for music tracks, for the tropical score. (5) Humanoid: an athletic build, swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 patterns), patterned shirts, shorts, six hair styles, a sun hat and a visor, an accessories hook, sandal straps, 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay, for the cast and the volleyball moves. (6) input.reserveCombo, so Create + Options stays the AI Hub\\'s. (7) touch: \\'never\\', for touch-only devices. (8) E.onStep, a hook that runs through fades and pauses, so the attract ends on any input.'],\n    ['If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', 'Make scenes fresh instances on every visit, or say loudly that they persist. Sticky scene state caused more game-breaking bugs than anything else.']\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    ['What did you like most about this prompt?', 'The definition of done, the testing rules (one command, only failures printed) and the exact exit-interview shape. Together with a detailed plan they left little to guess about what finished means.'],\n    ['What was confusing?', 'The plan\\'s file names and date came from a different run (claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749) than TASK.md\\'s (xhigh-20261103-225453); TASK.md winning settled it. Usage asks for cost and token estimates the model cannot see.'],\n    ['What was the hardest thing to do?', 'The breadth with no stubs allowed: a match engine, a five-day vacation sim, three casino games, Pool Hop, postcards, a story with two endings, local and online multiplayer, an attract mode and dev tools, all real and tested. Balancing the volleyball AI so rallies felt right took the most iterations.'],\n    ['What would you change in this prompt?', 'Let the plan derive its paths and dates from TASK.md instead of hard-coding them, say how to report Usage when no meter exists, and either give a time budget or allow the core to ship first, with the rest flagged as follow-ups.']\n  ]\n};\nG.aboutPages = () => [\n  { title: 'Credits', text: G.ABOUT.credits },\n  { title: 'Source Information', text: G.ABOUT.source.map(([k, v]) => k + ': ' + v).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Testing Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.testing.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Engine Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.engine.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Prompt Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.prompt.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') }\n];\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":5,"newStart":1,"newLines":5,"lines":[" /* 55-about: the About & Credits pages: Credits, Source Information, and the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback (the","- * same text as the build's final report and exit-interview.json). */","+ * same text as the build's final report and exit-interview.json, which tools/interview.mjs writes from G.ABOUT). */"," G.ABOUT = {","   credits: [","     'Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball is an original parody-tribute inspired by Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003). No assets from it are used. Every character, line, swimsuit, song and sound here is new.',"]},{"oldStart":7,"oldLines":11,"newStart":7,"newLines":45,"lines":["     'Design, code, art, music and writing: Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5), building from PLAN.md.',","     'Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), modified (see Engine Feedback).',","     'Motion clips via the engine\\'s mocap sets: Quaternius Universal Animation Library (CC0), Mesh2Motion (CC0) and the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses).',","-    'Online: the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub, lib/hub).',","+    'Online: the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub, lib/hub), unmodified.',","     'All music, sound effects and voices are synthesized in code at run time. No audio files.'","   ].join('\\n'),","-  source: [],","-  testing: [], engine: [], prompt: []","+  source: [","+    ['Date', '2026-11-03 22:54:53'],","+    ['Source', 'Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003)'],","+    ['Plan', 'PLAN.md'],","+    ['Genre and Perspective', 'Sports: arcade 2-on-2 beach volleyball, with a light life-sim vacation (calendar, friendships, gifts, collecting) and minigames (rhythm Pool Hop, casino). Matches use a side-on TV Broadcast camera (a slight 12-degree yaw, pitch 30) and a behind-the-team Serve Cam; places use a top-down three-quarter view; the island map is isometric; shops and tables are full-screen pixel UI.'],","+    ['Tech', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, patched: see Engine Feedback), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, Canvas 2D (WebGPU lighting where the browser has it), the engine\\'s Mocap player with a cut set of 31 clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Built and tested with Node 24, Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) and Python 3\\'s http.server for the hub harness. One self-contained HTML file, no network at run time.'],","+    ['Art Direction', 'The engine\\'s native PS1-era pixel art: HD Humanoid rigs in a new athletic build, dressed in procedurally drawn swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 pixel patterns) and accessories; resort props written as data shapes; time-of-day skies, sea and lighting; a gold-and-teal 10-foot pixel UI with hand-drawn button glyphs.'],","+    ['Animation', 'The engine\\'s procedural Humanoid rig, extended with 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay driven by the volleyball simulation (a spike\\'s jump starts at its contact time minus its rise), plus 31 motion-capture clips played through Mocap.drive for celebrations, idles, dances, swimming and reactions.'],","+    ['Audio', 'All in code with Web Audio: 9 original songs from a chord-aware song builder (steel, marimba, bell, organ and pluck instruments; a rally drum layer; a tempo lift at match point; key changes), 10 jingles, about 60 sound effects, ambience loops (waves, wind, crowd, pool, casino, crickets) and Animalese-style gibberish voices synthesized letter by letter for every speaker.'],","+    ['Online', 'AI Hub sessions (shared world, 1-4 players): the game\\'s own card, one match on the hub\\'s clock, host-authoritative rules and AI, every copy flying the ball from the same launches, client-judged touches the host validates, snapshots for positions, CPUs for empty or left seats, finish() with standings, awards and stats.'],","+    ['Testing', 'One command (node test/run.mjs) that prints only failures and a count: 19 suites and about 760 checks. Headless Node runs with game.advance and game.debug: units (ball flight, rules, judge, data), AI-vs-AI match statistics, the vacation, shops, story, minigames, UI, attract, dev mode, a fake-hub netcode test at 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and a scripted playthrough of the whole vacation as all six fighters to both endings. Playwright from file:// (a tour, the attract, an emulated touch-only phone), the engine\\'s tools/check.mjs, the AI Hub harness on 127.0.0.1 (4 scenarios), and screenshots and rig sheets for art.'],","+    ['Research', 'No web research. Read the engine handoff (API card, guide, index, asset card), the sports kit, the engine source for the Humanoid, Dialog, audio, input and Shell, the AI Hub protocol README, client and harness checks, and the engine repository\\'s motion-capture catalogs. The source game was described from general knowledge, by the plan.'],","+    ['Environment', 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown'],","+    ['Model', 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh'],","+    ['Usage', 'unknown (no cost or token meter was available to the model; the build session ran from about 22:55 on 2026-11-03 to about 01:00 on 2026-11-04, roughly two hours)']","+  ],","+  testing: [","+    ['How many issues did testing catch?', 'About 30. Scripted tests caught about 20 (rallies that never ended because the AI dug everything, a perfect bot losing because the CPU spiked into the net, a starter partner never assigned, scene state that stuck between visits, Pool Hop\\'s beat clock, the attract missing input during fades, a touch flag set only in browsers, a name clash between two modules) and screenshots about 10 (night lighting, overlapping text and panels, tiny text in the engine\\'s HTML menus, off-screen dev sliders).'],","+    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'Six: the Welcome Match never starting for Ingrid (no one liked her enough to partner up), the game freezing after the Welcome Match (a cutscene flag left set from the arrival), Pool Hop unwinnable when entered from the pool, the Final never being called on a second vacation, the credits never ending on a second run, and the title crashing while drawing its prompt in the browser (a name clash).'],","+    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: six full vacations, one per fighter, from the invitation through every day\\'s events, matches, minigames, shopping and gifts to both endings, the credits and the results. It found most of the critical bugs, all of them state that only breaks on a later step or a second run.'],","+    ['What was the least helpful test you ran?', 'The first version of \"no match phase stuck over 15 seconds\". It flagged long but legal rallies, not stuck states, until I redefined it as 15 seconds with no touch and no phase change.']","+  ],","+  engine: [","+    ['What did you like most about the engine?', 'That the whole game runs headless in exact fixed steps. A full vacation, four networked copies of a match and the attract all run in Node in seconds and replay exactly, and the Humanoid is drawn from math, so new outfits and poses are code, not art files.'],","+    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'A few times. The Shell theme\\'s font is a font family, not a CSS shorthand (the menus came out tiny). Mocap is a top-level const, not window.Mocap. game.go with a fade switches scenes later, so tests must wait. Scene objects are reused on every visit, so flags left in them break the next visit. And at yaw 0 the Broadcast view saw the net edge-on.'],","+    ['What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', 'The volleyball simulation: closed-form ball flight with precomputed net and landing events, contact planning (who plays the ball, when, where and at what height), the timing judge, the AI with its error model, and a design where the same launches drive local and online play.'],","+    ['What was the hardest part about using the engine?', 'Dressing the Humanoid in swimsuits. Its torso is one polygon in one color, so cuts and patterns needed a per-pixel torso fill inside the engine\\'s draw code, kept in step with its depth sorting and shading.'],","+    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', '(1) PlayStation symbols, arrows, accents and punctuation in the pixel font, for prompts and the writing. (2) Button symbols as a Game setting, PlayStation by default with Xbox as the option. (3) A per-letter dialog voice hook, for the Animalese voices. (4) Audio: a voice bus and Voices volume, audio.bus(), PeriodicWave instruments, and decay, filter and detune for music tracks, for the tropical score. (5) Humanoid: an athletic build, swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 patterns), patterned shirts, shorts, six hair styles, a sun hat and a visor, an accessories hook, sandal straps, 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay, for the cast and the volleyball moves. (6) input.reserveCombo, so Create + Options stays the AI Hub\\'s. (7) touch: \\'never\\', for touch-only devices. (8) E.onStep, a hook that runs through fades and pauses, so the attract ends on any input.'],","+    ['If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', 'Make scenes fresh instances on every visit, or say loudly that they persist. Sticky scene state caused more game-breaking bugs than anything else.']","+  ],","+  prompt: [","+    ['What did you like most about this prompt?', 'The definition of done, the testing rules (one command, only failures printed) and the exact exit-interview shape. Together with a detailed plan they left little to guess about what finished means.'],","+    ['What was confusing?', 'The plan\\'s file names and date came from a different run (claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749) than TASK.md\\'s (xhigh-20261103-225453); TASK.md winning settled it. Usage asks for cost and token estimates the model cannot see.'],","+    ['What was the hardest thing to do?', 'The breadth with no stubs allowed: a match engine, a five-day vacation sim, three casino games, Pool Hop, postcards, a story with two endings, local and online multiplayer, an attract mode and dev tools, all real and tested. Balancing the volleyball AI so rallies felt right took the most iterations.'],","+    ['What would you change in this prompt?', 'Let the plan derive its paths and dates from TASK.md instead of hard-coding them, say how to report Usage when no meter exists, and either give a time budget or allow the core to ship first, with the rest flagged as follow-ups.']","+  ]"," };"," G.aboutPages = () => [","   { title: 'Credits', text: G.ABOUT.credits },"]}],"originalFile":"/* 55-about: the About & Credits pages: Credits, Source Information, and the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback (the\n * same text as the build's final report and exit-interview.json). */\nG.ABOUT = {\n  credits: [\n    'Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball is an original parody-tribute inspired by Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003). No assets from it are used. Every character, line, swimsuit, song and sound here is new.',\n    '',\n    'Design, code, art, music and writing: Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5), building from PLAN.md.',\n    'Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), modified (see Engine Feedback).',\n    'Motion clips via the engine\\'s mocap sets: Quaternius Universal Animation Library (CC0), Mesh2Motion (CC0) and the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses).',\n    'Online: the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub, lib/hub).',\n    'All music, sound effects and voices are synthesized in code at run time. No audio files.'\n  ].join('\\n'),\n  source: [],\n  testing: [], engine: [], prompt: []\n};\nG.aboutPages = () => [\n  { title: 'Credits', text: G.ABOUT.credits },\n  { title: 'Source Information', text: G.ABOUT.source.map(([k, v]) => k + ': ' + v).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Testing Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.testing.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Engine Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.engine.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') },\n  { title: 'Prompt Feedback', text: G.ABOUT.prompt.map(([q, a]) => q + '\\n' + a).join('\\n\\n') }\n];\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013kJm1FxLVtdrBhduBPQx8t","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
