{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghXTVcAj6neLRUNheM17","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built *Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball*. The single file `AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453.html` (2 MB) and `exit-interview.json` are in this folder's root. The interview parses and every field is filled; the dev files are in the matching subfolder.\n\nThe final run of `node test/run.mjs` passed: 762 checks, 0 failures, across 19 suites. That includes six scripted full vacations (one per fighter, to both endings), Chromium from `file://`, the engine's own checker and the AI Hub harness in all four scenarios. The game was only ever played by bots and scripts; no person has played it with a real controller yet.\n\nWhat's in it:\n- **The match:** 2v2 volleyball with one timed button and a timing ring. It has dives, shots, blocks, Mine/Yours calls, aiming by stick or mouse, every fault, partner chemistry, Broadcast and Serve Cam, Sonny's commentary, a crowd, celebrations and highlight replays.\n- **The vacation:** five days of three slots each, with weather, moods and NPC teams. Five walkable places plus the Dock for the arrival, gifts, team-ups and break-ups, three shops (21 suits, 10 accessories, 25 gifts) and a wardrobe with try-on.\n- **Minigames and extras:** Pool Hop on the music's beat, the casino (cashier, prize counter, slots tuned to about 92% return, blackjack, roulette), and postcards with an album.\n- **Story:** the invitation through the Welcome Match, the daily events, the semifinal and the final against Sonny and Pepper with its specials. Then the party, two endings, credits, results and New Vacation+.\n- **Everything else:** saves, Beach Match for 1–4 local players, AI Hub online play with the game's own card, the attract mode, Dev Mode with live sliders, and PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option. Touch-only devices get the required notice. All audio is made in code.\n\nFollowing STAGE.md, the file and folder names and the date come from TASK.md; the plan was written for a different run. I changed the engine in eight places, listed under Engine Feedback below.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 22:54:53\n- **Source:** Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: sports (arcade 2-on-2 beach volleyball), plus a light life-sim vacation and minigames.\n  - Matches: a side-on TV Broadcast camera (12° yaw, pitch 30) and a behind-the-team Serve Cam.\n  - Places use a top-down three-quarter view, the island map is isometric, and shops and tables are full-screen pixel UI.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, patched), plain JavaScript and Canvas 2D, with WebGPU lighting where the browser has it.\n  - The engine's motion-capture player with 31 clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU) and the AI Hub session client 1.4.0.\n  - Built and tested with Node 24, Playwright 1.63.0 and Python 3's http.server; no network at run time.\n- **Art Direction:**\n  - The engine's native PS1-era pixel art: its rigs in a new athletic build, wearing swimsuits drawn in code (7 cuts, 14 patterns) and accessories.\n  - Resort props written as data, skies and lighting by time of day, and a gold-and-teal pixel UI with hand-drawn button glyphs.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rig, extended with 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay driven by the ball simulation, plus 31 motion-capture clips for celebrations, idles, dances and swimming.\n- **Audio:**\n  - Music: 9 original songs from a chord-aware song builder, with a rally drum layer and a tempo lift at match point.\n  - Effects: 10 jingles, about 60 sound effects and ambience loops.\n  - Voices: Animalese-style gibberish, made letter by letter for every speaker.\n- **Online:**\n  - AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, opening on the game's own card.\n  - The host runs the rules and the AI; every copy flies the ball from the same launches on the hub clock.\n  - Each player judges her own touches and the host validates them; CPUs fill empty or abandoned seats.\n- **Testing:**\n  - One command that prints only failures and a count: 19 suites, about 760 checks.\n  - Headless: unit tests, AI match statistics, every system, and a fake-hub netcode test at 150 ms latency with jitter and 5% loss.\n  - Six full scripted vacations; Playwright from `file://` (a tour, the attract mode, an emulated touch-only phone); the engine's checker; the AI Hub harness on 127.0.0.1; screenshots and rig sheets for the art.\n- **Research:** none on the web. I read the engine handoff (API card, guide, index, asset card), the sports kit, the engine source I patched, the AI Hub protocol, client and harness, and the motion-capture catalogs. The source game was described from general knowledge, as in the plan.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** unknown; I have no cost or token meter. The session ran from about 22:55 on Nov 3 to about 01:00 on Nov 4, roughly two hours.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. Scripted tests caught about 20:\n   - rallies that never ended because the AI dug every ball;\n   - a \"perfect\" bot that lost because the CPU spiked into the net;\n   - a starter partner who was never assigned;\n   - scene state that stuck between visits;\n   - Pool Hop's beat clock;\n   - the attract mode missing input during scene fades;\n   - a name clash between two modules.\n\n   Screenshots caught about 10: night lighting, overlapping panels and text, tiny text in the engine's HTML menus, and dev sliders drawn off-screen.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** Six:\n   - the Welcome Match never started when playing as Ingrid;\n   - the game froze after the Welcome Match;\n   - Pool Hop was unwinnable when entered from the pool;\n   - the Final was never called on a second vacation;\n   - the credits never ended on a second run;\n   - the title screen crashed while drawing its prompt.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of six full vacations. It found most of the critical bugs, which only broke on a later step or a second run.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the first version of \"no match phase stuck over 15 s\". It flagged long but legal rallies until I redefined it as 15 seconds with no touch and no phase change.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the whole game runs headless in exact fixed steps. A full vacation, four networked copies of a match and the attract mode all run in Node in seconds and replay exactly. And because the character rig is drawn from math, new outfits and poses are code, not art files.\n2. **Where I got stuck or guessed wrong:**\n   - The HTML menu theme's `font` is a font family, not a CSS shorthand, so the menus came out tiny.\n   - `Mocap` is a top-level const, not a `window` property.\n   - Scene changes with a fade happen later, so tests have to wait for them.\n   - Scene objects are reused on every visit, so a flag left set breaks the next visit.\n   - At yaw 0 the Broadcast camera saw the net edge-on.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the volleyball simulation. It covers ball flight in closed form, contact planning, the timing judge and the AI. It's designed so the same launches drive both local and online play.\n4. **The hardest part:** dressing the rig in swimsuits. Its torso is one single-color polygon, so cuts and patterns needed a per-pixel fill inside the engine's draw code.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   1. New pixel-font glyphs: PlayStation symbols, arrows, card suits, accents and punctuation, for prompts and the writing.\n   2. Button symbols as a Game setting, PlayStation by default with Xbox as the option.\n   3. A per-letter dialog voice hook, for the Animalese voices.\n   4. Audio: a voice bus with a Voices volume, instruments built from harmonics, and decay, filter and detune for music tracks, for the tropical score.\n   5. The character rig:\n      - an athletic build;\n      - swimsuits, patterned shirts and shorts;\n      - six hair styles, a sun hat and a visor;\n      - an accessories hook and sandal straps;\n      - 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay, for the volleyball moves.\n   6. Holding Create blocks Options on a controller, because Create + Options belongs to the AI Hub.\n   7. A no-touch mode for touch-only devices.\n   8. A hook that runs every step, through fades and pauses, so the attract mode ends on any input.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make scenes fresh instances on every visit, or document clearly that they persist. Sticky scene state caused more game-breaking bugs than anything else.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the definition of done, the testing rules (one command, only failures printed) and the exact exit-interview shape. With a detailed plan, they left little to guess about what \"finished\" means.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - The plan's file names and date came from a different run than TASK.md's; TASK.md winning settled it.\n   - Usage asks for cost and token estimates I can't see.\n3. **The hardest thing:** the breadth with no stubs allowed: a match engine, a vacation sim, three casino games, Pool Hop, postcards, a branching story, local and online multiplayer, an attract mode and dev tools, all real and tested. Balancing the AI so rallies felt right took the most iterations.\n4. **What I'd change:**\n   - Have the plan take its paths and date from TASK.md instead of hard-coding them.\n   - Say how to report Usage when no meter exists.\n   - Either give a time budget, or allow the core to ship first with the rest flagged as follow-ups."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1695,"cache_read_input_tokens":944401,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1695},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"1419d1ad-f810-4c23-bfe0-5329cdb182c8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:53:37.660Z","request_id":"req_011CghXTV7eTdP1zzfusHmnt"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied. The html and exit-interview.json exist and parse, and the final reply has all four sections. But the transcript shows gaps against TASK.md: the About text claims 'about 760 checks' and per-claim figures the transcript never measured (e.g. 'about 30 issues', 'about 60 sound effects', 'roughly two hours'); the final reply's 'Testing Feedback' and 'Engine Feedback' answers are said to be 'about' counts; the game's 'Clean' mode is unverified, and PlayStation glyph behavior with a real pad was never tested (only headless). Those are honesty issues rather than missing features. The stronger issue is 'every feature in TASK.md implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs': the transcript never verified several TASK.md items end to end, including the 10-second attract playlist montage beats as captured in screenshots, the Serve Cam view on a live match, and the 4-player Beach Match on real pads. The test run passing does not establish those, and the transcript shows at least one mid-session bug class (scene state persisting) that tests only caught after the fact. Therefore the feature-completeness condition is not clearly evidenced."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8aedb0d-fc32-4598-b2c3-8a34e0c85184","uuid":"b1c3f332-1788-4473-af25-fa6c971f02e0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:53:46.656Z","isSynthetic":true}
